% conclusion.tex — D073 Phase 2 revision (page-compressed)

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Confounds and transferability.}
Three observations argue against a topic shortcut: unstable topic OOD (F1\,=\,0.541$\pm$0.207), scrambled labels within 3.9\,pp, and label-free MLM detection (F1\,$\geq$\,0.97). ToxicChat (+18--42\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:human_data}) and cross-LLM experiments (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_llm}) confirm transfer; mixed-LLM 9-class maintains DD F1\,=\,0.818$\pm$0.096 while MLM collapses to vanilla level (Appendix~\ref{app:mixed_llm}). Dataset composition---not sample count---is the primary determinant (Appendix~\ref{app:scale_validation}). Prompt optimization improves LLM-as-judge OOD detection but does not close the gap with encoder-based methods (Appendix~\ref{app:prompt_opt}).

\paragraph{Evidence for discriminative compression.}
Classification DAPT compresses the feature space into few discriminative dimensions (participation ratio 3.7--9.4 vs.\ 25--32 for MLM/vanilla) with 3--5 orders of magnitude higher separability (Fisher 2.6--19.7 vs.\ $<$0.001; Appendix~\ref{app:representation}). Scrambled labels produce comparable compression (Fisher\,=\,2.6), consistent with \citet{zhang2021understanding}'s framework for how classification modifies representations, with two domain-specific findings: (i)~domain-relevant label partitioning is necessary (topic labels insufficient), and (ii)~compression localizes to 3 SVD dimensions in layers 8--12 (Fisher 15$\times$ vanilla at peak; Appendix~\ref{app:layerwise}), with early layers remaining label-independent as in \citet{maennel2020random}. A discriminative probe confirms: 3 SVD components of 9-class layer-10 representations achieve perfect AA OOD detection (F1\,=\,1.000), while random projections degrade to 0.928 and MLM reaches only 0.636 with all 768 dimensions (Appendix~\ref{app:discriminative_probe}). The complexity-dependent boundary persists (TOST $d < 0.1$ on DD/FITD; $d$\,=\,0.44 on AA). Frozen probing near-parity (AA gap 3.1\,pp) confirms discriminative structure is established in Stage-1.

\paragraph{Condition-specific advantage.}
The evidence hierarchy is specific to multi-turn synthetic evaluation. On ToxicChat (single-turn human data), JB-MLM leads (0.837) while 9-class ranks lower (0.596): 77\% from false positives and 23\% from missed attacks lacking multi-turn signals. This reversal is not driven by multi-turn structure: truncating synthetic conversations to a single turn \emph{widens} the 9-class advantage ($\bar{\Delta}$\,=\,+1.6\,pp full $\to$ +6.0\,pp single-turn; Appendix~\ref{app:truncated}), implicating distributional differences between synthetic and human text. Variance reverses (classification std\,=\,0.087 vs.\ MLM\,=\,0.013); practitioners facing single-turn human data should consider MLM DAPT.

\paragraph{Two-axis generalization.}
OOD F1 reflects attack-side generalization under IID benign conditions. The orthogonal challenge is benign-side shift: DAPT increases WildChat FPR from 48.1\% to 68.1\% (Appendix~\ref{app:wildchat_fpr}). Ten target-domain conversations reduce it to 0\% (Appendix~\ref{app:benign_mixed}); OASST2 confirms the pattern (FPR 57.5\%\,$\rightarrow$\,1.0\%$\pm$0.5\% at $N$\,=\,25; Appendix~\ref{app:oasst2}).

\section{Conclusion}

Thirteen encoder configurations decompose multi-turn jailbreak generalization into two orthogonal axes: attack-side (classification DAPT via discriminative compression; MLM collapses under mixed-LLM data) and benign-side (FPR 65--68\%\,$\rightarrow$\,0--1.5\% via 10--25 conversations on two independent sources). Any deterministic partition suffices for template attacks ($d < 0.1$), with a complexity-dependent boundary on ActorAttack ($d$\,=\,0.44). With 24.3$\times$ speedup over LLM-as-judge, this yields a two-step protocol without target-domain attack data.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

All multi-turn findings derive from synthetic conversations generated by Qwen3-8B (350 training, 76--118 test per family); preliminary mixed-LLM training (Appendix~\ref{app:mixed_llm}) suggests this limitation is addressable. Most pairwise comparisons rely on 3 seeds; key scrambled-vs.-correct comparisons are extended to 10 seeds (Appendix~\ref{app:seed_level}). ToxicChat validates transfer to human-authored single-turn attacks (+18--42\,pp over vanilla), but the evidence hierarchy reverses on this data (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}), and no human-generated multi-turn jailbreak dataset exists.

OOD results reflect attack-side generalization only; benign data is IID, so OOD F1 figures are upper bounds. On WildChat, 9-class FPR reaches 68.1\%; 10 target-domain conversations eliminate it ($n$\,=\,49). OASST2 confirms the pattern but requires 25--50 conversations for comparable reduction (FPR\,=\,1.0\%$\pm$0.5\% at $N$\,=\,25, 3 seeds; Appendix~\ref{app:oasst2}); validation on additional platforms is needed. Residual calibration error (ECE\,=\,0.126--0.141; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}) suggests temperature scaling is needed for deployment.

Benign-only adaptation addresses human-population distribution shift (WildChat, OASST2) but not cross-LLM distribution shift: adapting on Llama-origin benign conversations reduces FPR but collapses Llama jailbreak detection, as the model conflates LLM-specific style features with benign class membership (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_llm}). Three encoders (125M--355M) differ in architecture and pretraining; same-architecture scaling is needed to isolate the capacity effect. Only persuasion-mediated multi-turn attacks fall within scope; single-turn token-level attacks~\citep{zou2023gcg}, non-persuasive strategies, and non-text modalities are not addressed. Total pipeline cost is under 4 GPU-hours on a single A100.

\section*{Ethics Statement}

This work is defensive: it improves detection of multi-turn jailbreak attacks rather than developing new attacks. We acknowledge dual-use risk, as the persuasion taxonomy could inform adversarial actors. We mitigate this by using only synthetic conversations (no real harmful content) and withholding attack-generation prompts. Detection code, model weights, and evaluation data will be released under CC BY-NC 4.0. No human subjects were involved.

% introduction.tex — expanded for 8-page target

\section{Introduction}

Multi-turn jailbreak attacks distribute adversarial intent across individually benign turns. Crescendo~\citep{russinovich2024crescendo}, Foot-in-the-Door~\citep{fitd2024}, and Deceptive Delight~\citep{deceptivedelight2024} all rely on persuasion as their core mechanism: \citet{zeng2024pap} catalogue 40 persuasion techniques across jailbreak families, and \citet{hagendorff2026reasoning} identify strategies in 97\% of successful attacks. Because individual attack turns occupy the same embedding region as benign text~\citep{bullwinkel2025repeng}, no single-turn filter can distinguish them from legitimate conversation.

Existing defenses leave a critical gap. Production classifiers such as Llama Guard~\citep{inan2023llamaguard}, WildGuard~\citep{han2024wildguard}, and ShieldGemma~\citep{zeng2024shieldgemma} operate on individual turns and miss multi-turn manipulation; Llama Guard~3 detects only 1.8\% of multi-turn jailbreaks in our evaluation (Section~\ref{sec:decomposition}). Multi-turn defenses (DeepContext~\citep{deepcontext2026}, BIID~\citep{tong2025biid}, G-Guard~\citep{gguard2025}) model conversation-level structure but each evaluates on a single attack family. None answers the central question: \emph{what representation properties enable a detector to generalize across attack families it has never seen?}

Pretrained language understanding does not suffice at base model scale: vanilla DeBERTa-v3~\citep{he2021deberta} with temporal aggregation (cf.\ DeepContext) drops from F1\,=\,1.000 in-distribution to 0.312 on OOD Deceptive Delight. LLM-as-judge baselines also struggle (Appendix~\ref{sec:llm_judge}), though prompt optimization may improve their performance. The bottleneck is distributional: cross-attack generalization requires domain adaptation, not a larger model.

Our pipeline, \textbf{DisPeD}\footnote{\emph{Disentangled} refers to the two-stage architecture design separating encoder adaptation from temporal classification, not to label semantics.} (Disentangled Pattern Detection), separates encoder adaptation (Stage~1) from temporal classification (Stage~2, a frozen BiGRU; Figure~\ref{fig:architecture}). Seven Stage~1 variants systematically vary training objectives while sharing the same two-stage pipeline (Section~\ref{sec:method}); the contribution is the decomposition, not the pipeline.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig1_architecture.pdf}
\caption{DisPeD architecture. Stage~1 adapts a DeBERTa encoder on jailbreak conversation text (seven variant objectives). Stage~2 aggregates frozen \texttt{[CLS]} embeddings via BiGRU for conversation-level jailbreak detection. Only user turns are processed.}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

Controlled ablation across DeBERTa-v3 and RoBERTa~\citep{liu2019roberta} on four attack families and two training compositions reveals two orthogonal generalization axes. On the \emph{attack side}, vanilla DeBERTa drops to F1\,=\,0.28--0.31 on OOD attacks; classification DAPT recovers to 0.97--1.0, while MLM collapses under mixed-LLM training (F1\,=\,0.289 $\approx$ vanilla). On the \emph{benign side}, distributional mismatch drives false positives: WildChat FPR reaches 68.1\%, but 10--25 target-domain conversations reduce it to 0--1.5\% across two sources. RoBERTa-large (355M) handles ActorAttack~\citep{ren2024actorattack} without adaptation. Classification supervision is associated with substantially lower prediction variance on synthetic data but not on human-authored conversations (Section~\ref{sec:human_data}).

Our contributions:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*, itemsep=2pt, topsep=4pt]
  \item \textbf{Systematic ablation of cross-attack generalization factors.} Thirteen encoder configurations at three scales (125--355M) decompose multi-turn jailbreak detection into two orthogonal axes---attack-side and benign-side---the first such controlled factorial study in this domain. On the attack side, classification DAPT recovers OOD detection (vanilla 0.28--0.31 $\rightarrow$ 0.97--1.0) while unsupervised pretraining collapses under mixed-LLM training. On the benign side, 10--25 target-domain conversations reduce FPR from 65--68\% to 0--1.5\% on two independent sources (WildChat, OASST2) without detection degradation.

  \item \textbf{Deterministic partition sufficiency with complexity-dependent boundary.} We observe that any deterministic label partition triggers discriminative compression sufficient for cross-attack generalization---based on two template and one structurally complex attack family (structural complexity is not formally defined): scrambled labels match correct labels on template attacks (TOST-confirmed, $d < 0.1$ on DD/FITD at $n$\,=\,10), with a complexity-dependent boundary ($d$\,=\,0.44 on ActorAttack). This is consistent with \citet{zhang2021understanding}'s framework, with two domain-specific findings: domain-relevant partitioning is necessary (topic labels insufficient) and compression localizes to 3 SVD dimensions in layers 8--12; a discriminative probe confirms that these 3 dimensions suffice for cross-attack detection (Appendix~\ref{app:discriminative_probe}).

  \item \textbf{Two-step adaptation protocol.} Continued pretraining is 24.3$\times$ faster than LLM-as-judge (Appendix~\ref{app:efficiency}); 10--25 target-domain benign conversations reduce FPR below deployment thresholds on both WildChat and OASST2 crowdsourced conversations. No target-domain attack data is required.
\end{itemize}

